# `tasks()` makes three claims that no *passing* block can make about itself,
# and this is the report all three write.
#
#  * **`everyOrder` reruns the body once per completion order.** A block asserts
#    at the end of its body, so it can say what the order was — but "there was a
#    sixth run" is a claim the sixth run is inside. The first block here is true
#    on runs one to five and false on run six, so this golden holding its failure
#    is the body having run six times, and a golden without it would be the
#    reruns having stopped early.
#  * **A fault ends the block at the task it names.** A task has no error channel
#    — `parallel` answers `[B]` and every `B` comes from the closure — so a task
#    the plan fails is a task that ends the run, which is what a task that died
#    would do for real. The report is where the reason the test wrote comes back.
#  * **A fault whose task is never reached fails the test**, which is E6's
#    post-condition (`buri_rt_test_leave`) at the third double.
#
# The failure/seed line D6 adds will land in this golden: the runtime records the
# order and the seed today (`testing.rs`'s `task_order_note`) and nothing prints
# it yet, so re-blessing this case is part of that slice.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "everyOrder reruns the body, and a task the plan fails ends the block"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/fan", "--force", "--output=js"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "fail.txt"
  stream: OUT
}
